and welcoming back to the program today  Cory Dogen who's professor of sociology  at Stone Hill College also author of the  book kill it to save it recently  available in paperback Corey last time  you were on we talked a lot about well  many different things including the idea  of the privatization of different areas  of government under the pretense of  saving some of those institutions and  recently we've had this sort of come up  again when thinking about the VA and the  fired VA secretary saying that part of  the reason that he was pushed out was  his lack of desire to go in the  privatization direction which the Trump  administration seemed to want we've seen  Trump be critical of the united states  postal service another favorite of the  right to talk about privatization what  do you think is really the catalyst for  this desire to privatize is it the  possibility of privatizing in a way that  does crony capitalist handouts or is it  sort of the moral idea of private is  better than public yeah well that's a  great question and a great way to frame  it because I would argue as I do in the  book that it's both and what what the  book really tries to explain is how the  what I consider killa to save it  mentality and framework for American  policymaking today is in fact crony  capitalism with the kind of added  ideological and cultural component that  it tries to convince people that it's  actually better for them and so on the  one hand the privatization of the public  sector is this attempt to just wrench  out whatever profit is still possible  from the public sector privatizing  everything that works and everything  that doesn't work for the sake of  handouts to corporate capital but at the  same time justifying them through this  kind of morality play about hard work  who's deserving the corporate sector  knows better than the public sector how  to run things and that ideology is still  strong despite decades and decades of  fail  on the part of the private sector to run  public institution yeah it's amazing how  successful the talking point has been of  government is inefficient and I'd rather  things be handled by the private sector  as if it's some it's almost like a  dogmatic religious type belief when the  person is usually more nuanced right  like for example my my economic views  are most closely aligned with like the  well regulated social democracies of  Scandinavia which recognize that hey  capitalism for many areas does create  incentives for creativity and research  and development and innovation but not  in all areas do we want that market  system health care for example being one  of them education be one of them the  simple idea that private is always  better than public that government is  always inefficient even by some on the  left has been accepted to some degree  and I find that very well that's right  no I agree and you know I think that in  the past you know you and I probably  could have had an interesting  conversation where we might actually  disagree on whether or not the public  sector could inspire that level of  creativity and whether or not capitalism  was necessary at all but that kind of  conversation between people on the left  and the further left has been so drowned  out by the rightward movement of our  politics that it was you know and and  and I blame Clinton as much as Reagan  for this that the idea of triangulation  strategies on the part of the Democrats  was such that they bought into that  ideology that the private sector  actually could run things better and  that the public sector needed to be  downsized and instead of improving and  and and trying to create a bureaucracy  that worked  the mantra became killed a bureaucracy  and so by killing the bureaucracy it  became a kind of ruse for the right  under which it privatization became the  narrative and as you suggest it's become  the narrative to such a degree that even  most Democrats can't run on trying  to recreate a public sector as the book  suggests we were actually moving in the  direction of many of the social welfare  democracies the welfare state of Western  Europe up until the 1960s and 1970s and  it was really the post-vietnam era and  eventually Reagan that saw us take this  great you turn away from a public sector  and a welfare state and towards what we  have now which is just in some ways the  the worst kind of you know banditry  capitalism yeah and we've even seen how  it has tricked or bamboozled or  convinced a lot of right-wing voters as  we've been talking about I mean at  minimum dating back to the 2010  successes of the tea party but certainly  well beyond into supporting candidates  who are straight up running on policies  that wouldn't be good for those voters  and and you know tax plans are an  example and you say well hold on a  second this tax plan wouldn't benefit  you it would benefit the very rich and  there's sort of this idea that well I  might be in that group someday as  statistically and unlikely as it is I  think it's similar with some of these  public institutions right which is  listen you'd benefit now from more  public health funding from whatever the  case may be and it's sort of the same  reaction like yeah well that may be in  the short term but the truth is that the  private sector is just more efficient  and I'd rather than run it it's an  amazing bamboozling of voters that's  happened I think that's true to an  extent and and the one thing I would I  would add as a caveat is that I think  we've seen in part with Donald Trump's  election although I think it's really  hard to take out the hardcore kind of  white supremacy that that really shape  shaped a lot of his his voting base but  regardless I think we also have to look  at the ways in which it may not simply  be a bamboozling of a of an electorate  that just was too easily swayed by by  falsehoods and by lies and and Bakke  news but it's also that they've tapped  in to this morality as you suggest and I  argue in the book that that morality  goes back to our  very beginnings as a nation that this  mythology about hyper individualism  about rugged burly guys it only let free  from public regulations and  responsibilities will succeed and that  that's what's always driven our country  to its great success is just mythology  but it's still deeply ingrained in our  core values and identity so so while I  do agree that there's a certain  bamboozling going on a lot of the  bamboozling is so hardwired into our  into our mythology and our identity that  it's very easily tapped by these  right-wing spin doctors and I think  maybe that's why the populist rhetoric  that we heard from both Bernie Sanders  and Donald Trump that's right it worked  in a sense even though there's of course  a very big difference between left-wing  and right-wing populism and there's a  night and day difference in terms of  what the legislative actions would have  been by someone like Bernie Sanders and  the more authoritarian actions that  we've seen under Donald Trump but I  think that if we go back and saw and  sort of considered how powerful the  populist sounding rhetoric was to both  voters that called themselves left and  right I think that's kind of a big  indication of the predisposition from  from people to be attracted so those  ideas given what we've seen in terms of  neoliberalism and corporatism for the  last 50 or more years would you say oh  absolutely and and you know as I say in  the book you know populism cuts both  ways and you know for me the weakness  and and the weakness of vision as well  as will of the Democrats really played  through in this election and so you know  that this was a moment where the kind of  left populism that Sanders represented  was right and I think right for for  winning an election and the idea that  that Clinton was the selection in part  because of a lot of really bad you know  institutionalized party politics and  power from the Democratic Party but also  the notion that Sanders wasn't electable  which you know leads us to the question  of you know could he have been any less  electable than Clinton so you know  the Democratic Party was was holdin to  its own set of neoliberalism and I think  was scared by the kind of left wing  populism that Sanders represented we'll  see whether the Democrats in 2020 2018  and then 2020 can welcome a much more  radical left vision the kind of public  welfare social welfare politics that you  were discussing earlier or they're gonna  remain these kinds of you know so-called  centrist Democrats who are afraid of  real change are you so I think there's  there's certainly more than two views on  this but two views about how Democrats  should run in 2018 and in 2020 would be  on the one hand those who say you need  to just run as far to the left as  possible as far to the left as the  Democratic Party will sort of allow  candidates to run and allow them to win  primaries and that unbalanced you're  going to do better that way there's  another approach which I find to be a  little more pragmatic and I think it's  it's maybe a little more thoughtful  which is you need to calculate what type  of candidate will do best depending on  where they're running so if you're  running against a corporate Democrat in  West Virginia versus against a  Republican in Texas versus who knows  someone in Vermont that there's sort of  different types of candidates and  approaches that make sense  do you subscribe more to one or the  other view or some third view I haven't  mentioned yeah I'm a big fan of third  views I would suggest that you know  there's there's a truth to both but  ultimately you know especially in  congressional elections at statewide  elections you know I'm a grassroots  organizer at heart and and and a  Democrat with a small D you know and a  socialist I guess with a big s but I  would suggest that you know the people  will have to decide this and too often  we leave it the party strategists and  party functionaries and one of the  things that came out of the last failed  election in 2016 we're all of these  memos from places in  Pennsylvania and Michigan and Wisconsin  from you know union workers and and and  other kind of local leaders who are  saying you know the Democrats you know  never took on the class argument and  they never took on the opportunity to  talk about a multicultural working class  as opposed to a much more focused  identity politics that seemed more for  marketing purposes than they did for you  know social change purposes I really  think the grassroots decide this in in  ways that are really important and so  you know I would suggest that that the  grassroots really is further to the left  among the Democrats they tend to isolate  them after elections they love them when  they're doing the organizing work and  they're the ones that eventually have to  determine what the ideology of the party  is going to be I think it's a much  further left ideology than the Democrats  who hold the power of the party are  probably willing to accept so I think  we'll see the Democrats do better I  think we'll see the Democrats be very  schizophrenic in many ways and so you'll  see the kinds of conservative Democrats  like more like Jones in Alabama and and  and some of the recent congressional  election special elections but I also  think we'll see some some really great  left candidates winning as well and I  think there's gonna be a little bit of a  you know not quite a civil war but  certainly a real power struggle in the  party come 2020 the book now available  in paperback is kill it to save it we've  been speaking with the book's author and  professor of sociology at Stone Hill  College kori Duggan kori great to talk  to you again great to talk to you David  thank you so much

